1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:07,965
The brush clears a rock face, and it is covered in writing — neat, deliberate lines of it, placed where a road passes.

2
00:00:12,367 --> 00:00:14,787
This was not carved for a temple or a tomb.

3
00:00:14,787 --> 00:00:21,413
It was carved to be read by whoever walked by, and that makes it one of the most useful objects in Indian history.

4
00:00:26,867 --> 00:00:30,465
The edicts are their own source, and the main one for this episode.

5
00:00:30,465 --> 00:00:34,751
Chinese travellers who came centuries later describe the pillars still standing.

6
00:00:34,751 --> 00:00:38,808
And the story of how they were read again is part of the evidence too.

7
00:00:43,167 --> 00:00:47,612
The edicts come in groups, and the grouping is what examiners ask about.

8
00:00:47,612 --> 00:00:52,179
Fourteen major rock edicts, found at sites around the edges of the empire.

9
00:00:52,179 --> 00:00:57,046
Minor rock edicts, shorter and often more personal, scattered more widely.

10
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:05,164
Major pillar edicts, carved on polished sandstone pillars, mostly in the Ganga plains.

11
00:01:05,164 --> 00:01:08,105
Minor pillar inscriptions at particular places.

12
00:01:08,105 --> 00:01:10,801
And cave inscriptions in the Barabar hills,

13
00:01:10,801 --> 00:01:18,208
which record caves given to the Ajivikas — the rival school from the last unit, granted shelter by a Buddhist king.

14
00:01:21,833 --> 00:01:24,497
They are not all in one script or one language.

15
00:01:24,497 --> 00:01:26,662
Most are in Prakrit written in Brahmi.

16
00:01:26,662 --> 00:01:32,567
In the north-west they are in Prakrit written in Kharoshthi, the script that developed from Aramaic.

17
00:01:35,567 --> 00:01:41,010
And at Kandahar there are versions in Greek and in Aramaic, so that people there could read them.

18
00:01:41,010 --> 00:01:47,976
A ruler who has his message translated for the edge of his territory is thinking about communication, not just display.

19
00:01:52,133 --> 00:01:55,588
Learn a handful of sites and you can answer most questions.

20
00:01:55,588 --> 00:01:56,740
Girnar in the west.

21
00:01:56,740 --> 00:02:04,340
Dhauli and Jaugada on the east coast, both in the Kalinga country — and both carry a special edict addressed to the officers there,

22
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:07,001
about governing conquered people justly.

23
00:02:11,733 --> 00:02:12,983
Kalsi in the north.

24
00:02:12,983 --> 00:02:16,731
Shahbazgarhi and Mansehra in the north-west, in Kharoshthi.

25
00:02:16,731 --> 00:02:21,966
Maski and several southern sites, which matter because they name him directly.

26
00:02:25,233 --> 00:02:27,605
And there is a detective story here.

27
00:02:27,605 --> 00:02:32,668
For centuries nobody could read Brahmi, and nobody knew who had written these.

28
00:02:32,668 --> 00:02:37,347
The script was deciphered in 1837 by James Prinsep, working in Calcutta.

29
00:02:37,347 --> 00:02:46,043
But the inscriptions mostly called their author Devanampiya Piyadasi — "beloved of the gods, of pleasing appearance" — not Ashoka.

30
00:02:49,233 --> 00:02:55,983
The identification was confirmed when edicts were found, including at Maski, that use the name Ashoka directly.

31
00:02:55,983 --> 00:03:01,948
A king who had been almost forgotten in India was read back into history from his own stones.

32
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:12,118
Devanampiya Piyadasi is the title the edicts mostly use; Ashoka is the name, confirmed by the inscriptions that carry it.

33
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:20,666
If a question gives you Piyadasi, it is testing whether you know they are the same man.

34
00:03:24,767 --> 00:03:27,638
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

35
00:03:27,638 --> 00:03:29,950
Two surfaces: rocks and pillars.

36
00:03:29,950 --> 00:03:34,453
Two main scripts: Brahmi, and Kharoshthi in the north-west.

37
00:03:40,133 --> 00:03:40,851
Dig Quiz.

38
00:03:40,851 --> 00:03:42,216
Pause and name it.

39
00:03:42,216 --> 00:03:49,685
First: the script, written right to left and developed from Aramaic, used for the north-western edicts.

40
00:03:49,685 --> 00:03:50,846
Kharoshthi.

41
00:03:54,733 --> 00:04:02,858
Second: the edict site on the east coast, in the Kalinga country, carrying a special edict on governing conquered people.

42
00:04:02,858 --> 00:04:05,156
Dhauli — Jaugada also counts.

43
00:04:08,033 --> 00:04:11,797
Third: the hills whose caves he granted to the Ajivikas.

44
00:04:11,797 --> 00:04:12,391
Barabar.

45
00:04:12,391 --> 00:04:18,369
In Mains, a standard question asks what the edicts tell us that other sources cannot.

46
00:04:23,533 --> 00:04:27,024
Say: a ruler's own voice, dated and placed, on policy,

47
00:04:27,024 --> 00:04:32,910
ethics and administration — and note that it is still a ruler's own account of himself.

48
00:04:38,033 --> 00:04:43,653
A rock edict takes its place in the gallery: the first Indian government document we can read.

49
00:04:46,833 --> 00:04:51,807
Next gallery: how the empire was actually run, and why it did not last.

50
00:04:51,807 --> 00:04:56,044
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

